          "solution": "<ol type=\"a\">\n<li><p>Choose \\(B&gt;0\\) with \\(|y_n|\\le B\\) for all \\(n\\). Given \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), choose \\(N\\in\\mathbb N\\) such that \\(n\\ge N\\) implies \\(|x_n|&lt;\\varepsilon/B\\). Then</p>\n\\[|x_ny_n|\\le B|x_n|&lt;\\varepsilon\\qquad(n\\ge N),\\]\n<p>so \\(x_ny_n\\to0\\).</p></li>\n<li><p>Take \\(x_n=1/n\\) and \\(y_n=n\\). Then \\(x_n\\to0\\), while \\(\\{y_n\\}\\) is unbounded by the Archimedean property. But \\(x_ny_n=1\\) for every \\(n\\), so the product does not tend to zero.</p></li>\n</ol>",
