          "graded": true,
          "points": 10,
          "statement": "<p>Suppose that \\(x_n\\to x\\) and \\(y_n\\to y\\). Prove that</p>\n\\[\n\\max\\{x_n,y_n\\}\\to\\max\\{x,y\\}\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\min\\{x_n,y_n\\}\\to\\min\\{x,y\\}.\n\\]\n<p><strong>Hint:</strong> Express the maximum and minimum of two real numbers in terms of their sum and the absolute value of their difference.</p>",
          "solution": "<p>Considering the cases \\(u\\ge v\\) and \\(u&lt;v\\) gives</p>\n\\[\\max\\{u,v\\}=\\frac{u+v+|u-v|}{2},\\qquad\n\\min\\{u,v\\}=\\frac{u+v-|u-v|}{2}.\\]\n<p>The limit laws and the absolute-value limit theorem give \\(x_n+y_n\\to x+y\\) and \\(|x_n-y_n|\\to|x-y|\\). Therefore</p>\n\\[\\max\\{x_n,y_n\\}=\\frac{x_n+y_n+|x_n-y_n|}{2}\n\\longrightarrow\\frac{x+y+|x-y|}{2}=\\max\\{x,y\\},\\]\n\\[\\min\\{x_n,y_n\\}=\\frac{x_n+y_n-|x_n-y_n|}{2}\n\\longrightarrow\\frac{x+y-|x-y|}{2}=\\min\\{x,y\\}.\\]",
