          "graded": true,
          "points": 10,
          "statement": "<p>Using the squeeze theorem and the special sequence limits proved in class, prove that</p>\n<ol type=\"a\">\n<li>\\(\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}(n^2+3n+1)^{1/n}=1\\).</li>\n<li>\\(\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}(2^n+3^n)^{1/n}=3\\).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Give explicit upper and lower bounds for each sequence.</p>",
          "solution": "<ol type=\"a\">\n<li><p>For \\(n\\ge1\\), \\(n^2\\le n^2+3n+1\\le5n^2\\). Taking positive \\(n\\)th roots gives</p>\n\\[(n^{1/n})^2\\le(n^2+3n+1)^{1/n}\\le5^{1/n}(n^{1/n})^2.\\]\n<p>Since \\(n^{1/n}\\to1\\) and \\(5^{1/n}\\to1\\), both bounds tend to \\(1\\). The squeeze theorem gives the first limit.</p></li>\n<li><p>For \\(n\\ge1\\), \\(3^n\\le2^n+3^n\\le2\\cdot3^n\\), so</p>\n\\[3\\le(2^n+3^n)^{1/n}\\le3\\cdot2^{1/n}.\\]\n<p>Since \\(2^{1/n}\\to1\\), both bounds tend to \\(3\\). The squeeze theorem gives the second limit.</p></li>\n</ol>",
