          "graded": true,
          "points": 10,
          "statement": "<p>Let</p>\n\\[\nx_n=(-1)^n\\left(1+\\frac1n\\right).\n\\]\n<p>For each \\(n\\in\\mathbb N\\), determine</p>\n\\[\na_n=\\sup\\{x_k:k\\geq n\\}\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nb_n=\\inf\\{x_k:k\\geq n\\}.\n\\]\n<p>Justify your formulas, distinguishing even and odd \\(n\\) as needed. Then use these tail bounds to compute</p>\n\\[\n\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}x_n\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}x_n.\n\\]",
          "solution": "<p>The even-indexed terms \\(x_{2j}=1+1/(2j)&gt;1\\) decrease, while the odd-indexed terms \\(x_{2j-1}=-1-1/(2j-1)&lt;-1\\) increase. Every tail contains both parities, so its maximum is its first even-indexed term and its minimum is its first odd-indexed term. Hence</p>\n\\[a_n=\\begin{cases}\n1+1/n,&amp;n\\text{ even},\\\\\n1+1/(n+1),&amp;n\\text{ odd},\n\\end{cases}\\qquad\nb_n=\\begin{cases}\n-1-1/(n+1),&amp;n\\text{ even},\\\\\n-1-1/n,&amp;n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\\]\n<p>Since</p>\n\\[1\\le a_n\\le1+\\frac1n,\\qquad -1-\\frac1n\\le b_n\\le-1,\\]\n<p>squeeze gives \\(a_n\\to1\\) and \\(b_n\\to-1\\). By the definitions,</p>\n\\[\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}x_n=1,\\qquad\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}x_n=-1.\\]",
