          "graded": true,
          "points": 10,
          "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\{x_n\\}\\) be a bounded sequence of real numbers. Prove that</p>\n\\[\n\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}(-x_n)=-\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}x_n\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}(-x_n)=-\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}x_n.\n\\]\n<p><strong>Hint:</strong> Compare the supremum of \\(\\{-x_k:k\\geq n\\}\\) with the infimum of \\(\\{x_k:k\\geq n\\}\\).</p>",
          "solution": "<p>For a nonempty bounded set \\(A\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\), write \\(-A=\\{-a:a\\in A\\}\\). The number \\(-\\inf A\\) is an upper bound for \\(-A\\). If \\(U\\) is any upper bound for \\(-A\\), then \\(-U\\) is a lower bound for \\(A\\), so \\(-U\\le\\inf A\\), or \\(U\\ge-\\inf A\\). Thus \\(\\sup(-A)=-\\inf A\\). Applying this identity to \\(-A\\) also gives \\(\\inf(-A)=-\\sup A\\).</p>\n<p>Apply these identities to \\(A_n=\\{x_k:k\\ge n\\}\\). With \\(a_n=\\sup A_n\\) and \\(b_n=\\inf A_n\\),</p>\n\\[\\sup\\{-x_k:k\\ge n\\}=-b_n,\\qquad\\inf\\{-x_k:k\\ge n\\}=-a_n.\\]\n<p>The sequences \\(\\{a_n\\}\\) and \\(\\{b_n\\}\\) are bounded, respectively nonincreasing and nondecreasing, so they converge by monotone convergence. Taking limits and using the scalar-multiple limit law gives</p>\n\\[\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}(-x_n)=-\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}b_n=-\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}x_n,\\]\n\\[\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}(-x_n)=-\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}a_n=-\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}x_n.\\]",
