          "graded": true,
          "points": 10,
          "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\{x_n\\}\\) and \\(\\{y_n\\}\\) be sequences of real numbers. Suppose that \\(\\{x_n\\}\\) is Cauchy and</p>\n\\[\\lim\\limits_{n\\to\\infty}(x_n-y_n)=0.\\]\n<p>Prove directly from the definition of a Cauchy sequence that \\(\\{y_n\\}\\) is Cauchy. Do not use the equivalence between Cauchy sequences and convergent sequences.</p>",
          "solution": "<p>Let \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\). Since \\(\\{x_n\\}\\) is Cauchy and \\(x_n-y_n\\to0\\), choose \\(N\\in\\mathbb N\\) such that</p>\n\\[|x_m-x_n|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3},\\qquad |x_n-y_n|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}\\qquad(m,n\\geq N).\\]\n<p>For \\(m,n\\geq N\\), the triangle inequality gives</p>\n\\[|y_m-y_n|\\leq|y_m-x_m|+|x_m-x_n|+|x_n-y_n|&lt;\\varepsilon.\\]\n<p>Thus \\(\\{y_n\\}\\) is Cauchy by definition.</p>",
