          "number": 2,
          "title": "Summable successive differences",
          "graded": true,
          "points": 10,
          "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\{x_n\\}\\) be a sequence of real numbers such that</p>\n\\[|x_{n+1}-x_n|\\leq\\frac{1}{2^n}\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\in\\mathbb N.\\]\n<ol type=\"a\">\n<li>Prove that \\(\\{x_n\\}\\) is Cauchy and hence converges.</li>\n<li>Let \\(L=\\lim\\limits_{n\\to\\infty}x_n\\). Prove that\n\\[|L-x_n|\\leq\\frac{1}{2^{n-1}}\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\in\\mathbb N.\\]</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Hint for (a):</strong> For \\(m&gt;n\\), write \\(x_m-x_n\\) as a finite sum of successive differences.</p>",
          "solution": "<ol type=\"a\">\n<li><p>For \\(m&gt;n\\), the triangle inequality and a finite geometric sum give</p>\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\n|x_m-x_n|&amp;\\leq\\sum_{k=n}^{m-1}|x_{k+1}-x_k|\\leq\\sum_{k=n}^{m-1}\\frac1{2^k}\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac1{2^{n-1}}\\bigl(1-2^{-(m-n)}\\bigr)&lt;\\frac1{2^{n-1}}.\n\\end{aligned}\\]\n<p>Given \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), choose \\(N\\) such that \\(2^{1-N}&lt;\\varepsilon\\). The bound is less than \\(\\varepsilon\\) whenever \\(m&gt;n\\geq N\\); the cases \\(n&gt;m\\) and \\(m=n\\) follow by symmetry and zero difference. Thus \\(\\{x_n\\}\\) is Cauchy and therefore converges in \\(\\mathbb R\\).</p></li>\n<li><p>Fix \\(n\\). Letting \\(m\\to\\infty\\) in part (a), the absolute-value limit theorem and preservation of inequalities give</p>\n\\[|L-x_n|=\\lim_{m\\to\\infty}|x_m-x_n|\\leq\\frac1{2^{n-1}}.\\]</li>\n</ol>",
