          "id": "pset5-3",
          "number": 3,
          "title": "Bounded partial sums and weighted series",
          "graded": true,
          "points": 10,
          "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\{x_n\\}\\) be a sequence of real numbers whose partial sums \\(s_n=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}x_k\\) form a bounded sequence.</p>\n<ol type=\"a\">\n<li>Prove that, for every \\(m\\geq2\\),\n\\[\\sum_{n=1}^{m}\\frac{x_n}{n}=\\frac{s_m}{m}+\\sum_{n=1}^{m-1}\\frac{s_n}{n(n+1)}.\\]</li>\n<li>Use part (a) and a telescoping sum to prove that\n\\[\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{x_n}{n}\\]\nconverges. You are not assuming that \\(\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}x_n\\) converges.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Hint for (a):</strong> Set \\(s_0=0\\) and write \\(x_n=s_n-s_{n-1}\\).</p>",
          "solution": "<ol type=\"a\">\n<li><p>Set \\(s_0=0\\). Since \\(x_n=s_n-s_{n-1}\\), shifting indices gives</p>\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{n=1}^{m}\\frac{x_n}{n}\n&amp;=\\sum_{n=1}^{m}\\frac{s_n}{n}-\\sum_{n=0}^{m-1}\\frac{s_n}{n+1}\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{s_m}{m}+\\sum_{n=1}^{m-1}s_n\\left(\\frac1n-\\frac1{n+1}\\right)\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{s_m}{m}+\\sum_{n=1}^{m-1}\\frac{s_n}{n(n+1)}.\n\\end{aligned}\\]</li>\n<li><p>Choose \\(B&gt;0\\) such that \\(|s_n|\\leq B\\) for every \\(n\\). For every \\(m\\), telescoping gives</p>\n\\[\\sum_{n=1}^{m}\\frac{|s_n|}{n(n+1)}\\leq B\\sum_{n=1}^{m}\\left(\\frac1n-\\frac1{n+1}\\right)=B\\left(1-\\frac1{m+1}\\right)\\leq B.\\]\n<p>These nonnegative partial sums are nondecreasing and bounded, so \\(\\sum s_n/[n(n+1)]\\) converges absolutely, hence converges. Also \\(|s_m/m|\\leq B/m\\to0\\), so \\(s_m/m\\to0\\) by squeeze. Passing to the limit in part (a) proves convergence, with</p>\n\\[\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{x_n}{n}=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{s_n}{n(n+1)}.\\]</li>\n</ol>",
