          "number": 4,
          "title": "Absolute convergence with bounded factors",
          "graded": true,
          "points": 10,
          "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\{x_n\\}\\) and \\(\\{y_n\\}\\) be sequences of real numbers. Suppose that \\(\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}|x_n|\\) converges and that \\(\\{y_n\\}\\) is bounded. Use the Cauchy criterion for series to prove that</p>\n\\[\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}x_ny_n\\]\n<p>converges absolutely.</p>",
          "solution": "<p>Choose \\(B&gt;0\\) with \\(|y_n|\\leq B\\) for every \\(n\\). Given \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), the Cauchy criterion for \\(\\sum|x_n|\\) gives \\(M\\in\\mathbb N\\) such that</p>\n\\[\\sum_{n=m+1}^{\\ell}|x_n|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{B}\\qquad(\\ell&gt;m\\geq M).\\]\n<p>For these indices,</p>\n\\[\\left|\\sum_{n=m+1}^{\\ell}|x_ny_n|\\right|=\\sum_{n=m+1}^{\\ell}|x_ny_n|\\leq B\\sum_{n=m+1}^{\\ell}|x_n|&lt;\\varepsilon.\\]\n<p>By the Cauchy criterion, \\(\\sum|x_ny_n|\\) converges. Hence \\(\\sum x_ny_n\\) converges absolutely.</p>",
